TY  - RPRT
AU  - Torul, Orhan
TI  - Bridging the Gap: Absolute and Relative Intergenerational Educational Mobility
PY  - 2025
UR  - https://orhantorul.com/research/btg/
KW  - Social Mobility
KW  - Educational Attainment
KW  - Intergenerational Persistence
KW  - Human Capital
KW  - Educational Expansion
AB  - Does educational expansion disrupt intergenerational transmission of advantage, or merely shift inequality upward while preserving relative positions? Using the World Bank’s Global Database on Intergenerational Mobility covering 140+ countries across five birth cohorts (1940s–1980s), this paper examines how absolute and relative educational mobility relate. Three findings emerge. First, a negative association exists between the intergenerational persistence coefficient and absolute upward mobility: a 10-percentage-point increase in absolute mobility associates with 5.5-percentage-point decrease in persistence, strengthening nearly fourfold from 1940s to 1980s cohorts. Second, variance-adjusted measures reveal relationships only through cohort interactions, indicating temporal dynamics mediate these associations. Third, profound gender differences characterize recent cohorts, with daughters demonstrating different mobility patterns than sons. These findings document robust descriptive regularities indicating that absolute and relative educational mobility move together under specific conditions. While these observational patterns do not identify causal disruption mechanisms, the temporal strengthening of the absolute-relative relationship is highly consistent with theories of equity-focused institutional disruption. Crucially, this relationship survives a barrage of demanding econometric tests, including country-specific cohort trends, correlated random effects, and precision weighting, establishing a stringent set of stylized facts that modern stratification theories must accommodate.
ER  - 
